{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"agent_message","text":"**What I did**\n\n- Added **Prisma ORM 8 (rc)** to the project with `npm install prisma@latest @prisma/orm-postgres@latest`.\n- Ran `npx prisma orm init --yes --target postgres --authoring psl` to scaffold a Prisma 8 project.\n- Updated the data contract in `src/prisma/contract.prisma`:\n  - `User` now has a unique `email` and optional `name`.\n  - `Post` now has `title`, optional `content`, a `published` flag defaulting to `false`, and a required `author` relation.\n- Regenerated the contract artifacts (`contract.json`, `contract.d.ts`) with `npx prisma contract emit`.\n- Created the database tables using `npx prisma db init --yes` (the `--yes` flag auto‑accepts prompts, matching the colleague’s “use `--yes` to avoid hanging” advice).\n- Added **`script.ts`** at the project root that:\n  1. Creates a user with two posts.\n  2. Retrieves every user with their posts via `include('posts')`.\n  3. Prints the result as formatted JSON.\n  4. Closes the Prisma client when possible.\n\n**Applied parts of the colleague’s note**\n\n- **Used the current npm version** (`prisma@8.0.0‑rc.33`) rather than the older 7 line.\n- **Ran `orm init` with `--yes`** to avoid interactive prompts that would stall the process.\n- Followed the **Prisma‑8 workflow** (`prisma contract emit` → `prisma db init`) as described.\n- Leveraged the **ORM query syntax** (`db.orm.public.User.create`, `db.orm.public.Post.create`, `include('posts')`) shown in the note’s examples."}}
